   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Phil,<div>You always provide informative and useful answers!</div><div><div>Thanks for being so helpful!</div><div><br></div><div>Best</div><div>Luis</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><br></div><div><br></div><br>Em terça-feira, 11 de dezembro de 2018 19:55:07 UTC-2, Phil Chalmers escreveu:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div>My first question is related to estimation. The Graded Model converges after 2 thousand iterations, but when I ask M2 statistics (and CFI, TLI, RMSEA), mirt gives me the following warning:</div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Error</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">:</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">The</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> following items have only one response category </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">and</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> cannot be estimated</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">:</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> y1 y2 y3 y4 y5 y6 y7 y8 y10 y12 y13 y14 y15 y16 y17 y18 y19 y20 y21 y22 y23 y24 y25 y26 y27 y28 y29 y30 y31 y32 y33 y34 y35 y36 y37 y38 y39 y40 y41 y42 y43 y44 y45 y46 y47 y48 y49 y50 y51 y52 y53 y54 y55 y56 y57 y58 y59 y60 y61 y62 y63 y64 y65 y66 y67 y68 y69 y70 y71 y72 y73 y74 y75 y76 y77 y78 y79 y80 y81 y82<br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">In</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> cov2cor</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">cross</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">/</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">N </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> outer</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">colMeans</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">dat</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">),</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> colMeans</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">dat</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)))</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">:</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>  diag</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(.)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> had </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">or</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> NA entries</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> non</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">finite result </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">is</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> doubtful<br></span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Error</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">:</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">Null</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> model did </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">not</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> converge </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">or</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">is</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,136)\">not</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> supported</span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>This must be because you used na.rm=TRUE in the M2() statistic. Given your study design, removing cases row-wise no doubt removed so much data that the tables of counts were collapsed to only a single response option per item. Hence, computing things like correlations and covariances makes no sense if in the subset of data a single item has only the response 0-0-0-0-0-0-0....-0-0 an nothing else (zero variance). </div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div></div><div>But these variables have three categories.  </div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> table</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">cg_items$y1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br><br> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">  </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">2</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> <br> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">5</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">14</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">53</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span></div></code></div><div><div>I really don&#39;t know how to interpret that. Could you please give me some insights? I&#39;m able to ask the model coefficients though.</div></div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>In addition to that, I read in an old message that you said </div><div>&quot;Moreover; once my model is estimated, the only way to compute fit indices such as RMSEA, CFI etc... is to impute the original dataset using the function imputeMissing ? Is this apporach correct ?</div><div><i>Correct. I can&#39;t see any other way to do this because these statistics are based on complete datasets</i>&quot;. </div><div><br></div><div>So, <b>can I assume that all IRT estimation need to use complete dataset</b>? </div><div>Link here: <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/mirt-package/missing$20complete$20cfi%7Csort:date/mirt-package/slssi-7_koA/N-bghDqJBgAJ\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!searchin/mirt-package/missing$20complete$20cfi%257Csort:date/mirt-package/slssi-7_koA/N-bghDqJBgAJ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473102180000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Ob7bzctt9w4Liv8iyfNrf\">https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/mirt-package/missing$20complete$20cfi%7Csort:date/mirt-package/slssi-7_koA/N-bghDqJBgAJ</a></div><div><br></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>That&#39;s correct. And the imputation approach that mirt uses is rather conservative. Multiple imputation from outside information would be better to help remove this issue, but that&#39;s an entirely different and difficult beast to tackle. </div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div></div><div><div><br></div><div>The third question is related to different results between mirt estimation and ltm estimation. <b>The correlation is negative (-.7) and I&#39;m having a hard time to understand that. </b></div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>The attached data is virtually the same I&#39;m using and the following code will make my question clear.</div><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">library</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">mirt</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>fit_mirt </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> mirt</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">cg_items</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> itemtype </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,136,0)\">&quot;graded&quot;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> technical</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">list</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">NCYCLES</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">2000</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">))</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>library</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">ltm</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>fit_ltm </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> grm</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">cg_items</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">IRT</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">param</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br><br>M2</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">fit_mirt</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> type</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,136,0)\">&quot;C2&quot;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> na</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">rm</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br><br>coefs </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> data</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">frame</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">coef</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">fit_mirt</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">IRTPars</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">FALSE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> simplify</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">))</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>coefs </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> cbind</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">coefs</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">coef</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">fit_ltm</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> standardized </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">=</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">))</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br>cor</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">coefs$items</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">.</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">a1</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">coefs$Dscrmn</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><div><br>Thank you for being so supportive of all the members of this forum.<br></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Simple, ltm didn&#39;t properly converge to the correct MLEs. This is a common and known issue with the ltm package that doesn&#39;t seem to exist in mirt (check out the log-likelihood values in your code, they are -15698.79 for ltm and -14606.65 for mirt....clearly, ltm got into a bad likelihood region and terminated wayyyy to early. And worse yet, without any warning). Here&#39;s a further discussion of the problem on CV: <a href=\"https://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/63891/is-r-output-reliable-specially-irt-package-ltm/95324#95324\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/63891/is-r-output-reliable-specially-irt-package-ltm/95324%2395324&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473102181000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1XSiQ1AJrh72mf2ZmfIW7l\">https://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/63891/is-r-output-reliable-specially-irt-package-ltm/95324#95324</a></div><div><br></div><div>Phil</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div></div><div>Luis</div><div><br></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;mirt-package&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a rel=\"nofollow\">mirt-package...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.goo